::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 8% growth? {
= 1.08#Correct. Growth uses 1 + r, so 1 + 0.08 = 1.08.
~ 0.08#This is only the rate, not the whole growth factor.
~ 0.92#This represents 8% decay.
~ 1.8#This represents 80% growth, not 8%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 15% decay? {
~ 1.15#This represents 15% growth.
= 0.85#Correct. Decay uses 1 - r, so 1 - 0.15 = 0.85.
~ 0.15#This is only the rate, not the remaining factor.
~ 1.85#This does not represent 15% decay.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A population of 400 grows by 5% each year. Which model fits? {
~ P(t) = 400(0.95)<sup>t</sup>#This models 5% decay.
~ P(t) = 1.05(400)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the initial value and factor.
= P(t) = 400(1.05)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial amount is 400 and 5% growth gives factor 1.05.
~ P(t) = 400 + 5t#This is linear growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A car worth 18000 loses 10% of its value each year. Which model fits? {
~ V(t) = 18000(1.10)<sup>t</sup>#This models 10% growth.
~ V(t) = 18000 - 10t#This subtracts 10 dollars per year, not 10%.
~ V(t) = 0.90(18000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the initial value and factor.
= V(t) = 18000(0.90)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 18000 and 10% decay gives factor 0.90.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>In A(t)=250(1.12)<sup>t</sup>, what does 1.12 mean? {
= 12% growth per time period#Correct. The factor 1.12 means the amount keeps 100% and gains 12% each period.
~ 12% decay per time period#A factor greater than 1 represents growth.
~ 112% decay per time period#The amount does not decay when the factor is greater than 1.
~ Initial amount of 1.12#The initial amount is 250.
}

